WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.020
Film Courage: What are three rules someone
needs to know about screenwriting?

00:00:03.020 --> 00:00:07.610
Dr. Ken Atchity, Producer/Author: Well three
rules, that’s just an arbitrary number but

00:00:07.610 --> 00:00:09.750
let’s go with it.

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:16.550
I think the first thing that a screenwriter
needs to know is that everything has to be

00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:17.960
connected to everything else.

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:23.189
That’s the biggest difference between a
screenplay and a novel in my mind.

00:00:23.189 --> 00:00:28.150
In a novel you can get away with saying something
on page one that doesn’t connect directly

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:31.850
to something on page 158 and so on.

00:00:31.850 --> 00:00:37.110
Anything you say in a screenplay has to connect
to anything else you say in a screenplay.

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:42.600
If it doesn’t, the audience is going to
go “You know what I don’t get is why that

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:46.989
guy is wearing a red baseball cap in the first
scene and then in the fourth scene he was

00:00:46.989 --> 00:00:48.879
wearing a blue baseball cap.

00:00:48.879 --> 00:00:51.370
And then after that he wasn’t wearing a
baseball cap.”

00:00:51.370 --> 00:00:55.570
Honestly, that’s the kind of thing people
say when they are having a drink or coffee

00:00:55.570 --> 00:00:57.109
after a movie right?

00:00:57.109 --> 00:01:00.620
So every single thing has to connect with
everything else.

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:06.890
It’s much more challenging because it’s
almost like building a building where if one

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:10.640
thing isn’t connected properly the whole
building can collapse.

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:13.280
So I think that’s the first thing a screenwriter
has to know.

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:21.090
And I think the second thing a screenwriter
should know is chronological order and logical

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:27.060
order and psychological order have absolutely
nothing to do with it.

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:29.920
The only thing that matters is dramatic order.

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:32.320
That’s all the audience cares about.

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:40.220
If you hook us properly, it doesn’t matter
where we go in the story after that hook because

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:41.220
we will figure it out.

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:43.620
We will be so hooked that we’ll figure it
out.

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:47.490
You don’t have to even say 10 years later
or 5 years earlier or whatever.

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:52.649
You can help to say that maybe but you don’t
have to because we’re not stupid.

00:01:52.649 --> 00:01:58.000
And that’s the third thing, the audience
is the main character in the story not the

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:05.149
characters and pleasing the audience is what
makes the great directors great is they know

00:02:05.149 --> 00:02:07.920
what the audience is waiting for.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:16.260
In THE BIRDS, what of my favorite examples
Alfred Hitchcock has Tippi Hendren walking

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:22.010
up the creaky wooden steps to the attic because
she hears a noise in the attic.

00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:28.060
And she is wearing underwear because women
are always wearing underwear in the last scenes

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:35.690
of a horror film like Sigourney Weaver in
ALIEN.

00:02:35.690 --> 00:02:43.980
So she’s going up there despite the fact
that she’s locked in this house alone because

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:52.830
she’s afraid of the birds who have tried
to get in through the windows so why in the

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:56.360
world would she go up those steps?

00:02:56.360 --> 00:02:57.360
Why would she do that?

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:03.400
So if you start thinking about the character
logic or psychological order, you don’t

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:05.070
get the whole thing.

00:03:05.070 --> 00:03:06.790
What is wrong with her?

00:03:06.790 --> 00:03:10.010
This is what you are thinking, you the audience
as you are watching this.

00:03:10.010 --> 00:03:17.490
And she gets half way up the steps and the
noise gets worse and she stops and listens

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:19.380
to the noise.

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:23.180
And why is she stopping?

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:29.280
Well she’s stopping because we need time,
the audience needs time to catch up with the

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:34.890
story because we need to remind ourself…we
need to first say all of these things about

00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:39.040
how stupid she is and why isn’t she wearing
clothes and why doesn’t she test the flashlight

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:42.780
that she got from behind the couch (she doesn’t
even turn it on, she’s just got it) and

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:48.300
of course at the top of the steps it’s not
going to work but that’s typical and we’re

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:50.270
going through all of this in our heads.

00:03:50.270 --> 00:03:55.440
But when she stops on the last step before
she goes to the door we are suddenly…all

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:58.030
of our inner dialogue has stopped.

00:03:58.030 --> 00:04:05.960
And we’re going “Okay, I paid actually
$22.00 to get scared out of my mind and that’s

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:06.960
where we are now.”

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:10.540
She’s going to open the door and I’m going
to be scared.

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:13.280
And when you are ready for that, then she
can open the door.

00:04:13.280 --> 00:04:17.620
A person who didn’t understand that the
audience is the most important character in

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:25.400
the story would have her walk up the steps,
would maybe have her test the flashlight,

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:28.180
would have a whole different way of doing
it.

00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:35.810
Everything that a good director does is based
on the audience, not based on the characters

00:04:35.810 --> 00:04:38.410
or what happens logically in the next order.

00:04:38.410 --> 00:04:41.490
It’s based on what the audience has paid
for.

00:04:41.490 --> 00:04:43.880
What are you hear for?

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:45.260
And am I going to lose you or not?

00:04:45.260 --> 00:04:48.880
Because on that middle step where she stops
“Do you want to leave the theater?

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:52.520
This is the last time we go to one of these
stupid movies…it’s really…”

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:54.010
Okay but get up and leave.

00:04:54.010 --> 00:04:59.000
But if you are still there then she goes up
another couple steps because he knows exactly

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:03.970
what is going through your mind and his job
is to get you to the point where your mind

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:07.880
is blank and you are just waiting to be scared
because that is what you paid for.

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:15.200
That’s what I mean by the psychology of
the audience.

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:20.280
That is I think the most of screenwriting
is knowing that.

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:23.080
What does the audience ant to see here.

00:05:23.080 --> 00:05:29.390
Not what do I have to do first and what do
I have to expose first, what kind of exposition

00:05:29.390 --> 00:05:30.390
do I need.

00:05:30.390 --> 00:05:34.630
It’s like how can I grab the audience by
the throat and never let go of it?

